## sn12.17
sn12.17:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: Thus have I heard.
sn12.17:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Rājagaha in the Bamboo Grove, the squirrels’ feeding ground. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was dwelling at Rajagaha in the Bamboo Grove, the Squirrel Sanctuary.
sn12.17:1.3 | S: Then the Buddha robed up in the morning and, taking his bowl and robe, entered Rājagaha for alms. | T: Then, in the morning, the Blessed One dressed and, taking bowl and robe, entered Rajagaha for alms.
sn12.17:1.4 | S: The naked ascetic Kassapa saw the Buddha coming off in the distance. | T: The naked ascetic Kassapa saw the Blessed One coming in the distance.
sn12.17:1.5 | S: He went up to the Buddha, and exchanged greetings with him. | T: Having seen him, he approached the Blessed One and exchanged greetings with him.
sn12.17:1.6 | S: When the greetings and polite conversation were over, he stood to one side and said to the Buddha, | T: When they had concluded their greetings and cordial talk, he stood to one side and said to him:
sn12.17:1.7 | S: “I’d like to ask the worthy Gotama about a certain point, if you’d take the time to answer.” | T: “We would like to ask Master Gotama about a certain point, if he would grant us the favour of answering our question.”
sn12.17:1.8 | S: “Kassapa, it’s the wrong time for questions. | T: “This is not the right time for a question, Kassapa.
sn12.17:1.9 | S: We’ve entered an inhabited area.” | T: We have entered among the houses.”
sn12.17:2.1 | S: A second time, | T: A second time and a third time the naked ascetic Kassapa said to the Blessed One:
sn12.17:2.2 | P: “puccheyyāma mayaṁ bhavantaṁ gotamaṁ kañcideva desaṁ, sace no bhavaṁ gotamo okāsaṁ karoti pañhassa veyyākaraṇāyā”ti. | T: “We would like to ask Master Gotama about a certain point, if he would grant us the favour of answering our question.”
sn12.17:2.3 | P: “Akālo kho tāva, kassapa, pañhassa; | T: “This is not the right time for a question, Kassapa. We have entered among the houses.”
sn12.17:2.4 | P: antaragharaṁ paviṭṭhamhā”ti. | T: (none)
sn12.17:2.5 | S: and a third time, Kassapa spoke to the Buddha and the Buddha replied. | T: (none)
sn12.17:2.6 | P: antaragharaṁ paviṭṭhamhāti. | T: (none)
sn12.17:2.7 | S: When this was said, Kassapa said to the Buddha, | T: Then the naked ascetic Kassapa said to the Blessed One:
sn12.17:2.8 | S: “I don’t want to ask much.” | T: “We do not wish to ask Master Gotama much.”
sn12.17:2.9 | S: “Ask what you wish, Kassapa.” | T: “Then ask what you want, Kassapa.”
sn12.17:3.1 | S: “Well, worthy Gotama, is suffering made by oneself?” | T: “How is it, Master Gotama: is suffering created by oneself?”
sn12.17:3.2 | S: “Not so, Kassapa,” said the Buddha. | T: “Not so, Kassapa,” the Blessed One said.
sn12.17:3.3 | S: “Then is suffering made by another?” | T: “Then, Master Gotama, is suffering created by another?”
sn12.17:3.4 | S: “Not so, Kassapa,” said the Buddha. | T: “Not so, Kassapa,” the Blessed One said.
sn12.17:3.5 | S: “Well, is suffering made by both oneself and another?” | T: “How is it then, Master Gotama: is suffering created both by oneself and by another?”
sn12.17:3.6 | S: “Not so, Kassapa,” said the Buddha. | T: “Not so, Kassapa,” the Blessed One said.
sn12.17:3.7 | S: “Then does suffering arise anomalously, not made by oneself or another?” | T: “Then, Master Gotama, has suffering arisen fortuitously, being created neither by oneself nor by another?”
sn12.17:3.8 | S: “Not so, Kassapa,” said the Buddha. | T: “Not so, Kassapa,” the Blessed One said.
sn12.17:3.9 | S: “Well, is there no such thing as suffering?” | T: “How is it then, Master Gotama: is there no suffering?”
sn12.17:3.10 | S: “It’s not that there’s no such thing as suffering. | T: “It is not that there is no suffering, Kassapa;
sn12.17:3.11 | S: Suffering is real.” | T: there is suffering.”
sn12.17:3.12 | S: “Then does the worthy Gotama not know or see suffering?” | T: “Then is it that Master Gotama does not know and see suffering?”
sn12.17:3.13 | S: “It’s not that I don’t know or see suffering. | T: “It is not that I do not know and see suffering, Kassapa.
sn12.17:3.14 | S: I do know suffering, | T: I know suffering,
sn12.17:3.15 | S: I do see suffering.” | T: I see suffering.”
sn12.17:4.1 | S: “Worthy Gotama, when asked these questions, you say ‘not so’. | T: “Whether you are asked: ‘How is it, Master Gotama: is suffering created by oneself?’
sn12.17:4.2 | P: ‘Kiṁ pana, bho gotama, paraṅkataṁ dukkhan’ti iti puṭṭho samāno ‘mā hevaṁ, kassapā’ti vadesi. | T: or ‘Is it created by another?’
sn12.17:4.3 | P: ‘Kiṁ nu kho, bho gotama, sayaṅkatañca paraṅkatañca dukkhan’ti iti puṭṭho samāno ‘mā hevaṁ, kassapā’ti vadesi. | T: or ‘Is it created by both?’
sn12.17:4.4 | P: ‘Kiṁ pana, bho gotama, asayaṅkāraṁ aparaṅkāraṁ adhiccasamuppannaṁ dukkhan’ti iti puṭṭho samāno ‘mā hevaṁ, kassapā’ti vadesi. | T: or ‘Is it created by neither?’ in each case you say: ‘Not so, Kassapa.’
sn12.17:4.5 | S: Yet you say that there is such a thing as suffering. | T: When you are asked: ‘How is it then, Master Gotama: is there no suffering?’ you say: ‘It is not that there is no suffering, Kassapa; there is suffering.’
sn12.17:4.6 | P: ‘Tena hi bhavaṁ gotamo dukkhaṁ na jānāti na passatī’ti iti puṭṭho samāno ‘na khvāhaṁ, kassapa, dukkhaṁ na jānāmi na passāmi. | T: When asked: ‘Then is it that Master Gotama does not know and see suffering?’ you say: ‘It is not that I do not know and see suffering, Kassapa.
sn12.17:4.7 | S: And you say that you do know suffering, | T: I know suffering,
sn12.17:4.8 | S: and you do see suffering. | T: I see suffering.’
sn12.17:4.9 | S: Sir, let the Blessed One explain suffering to me! | T: Venerable sir, let the Blessed One explain suffering to me.
sn12.17:4.10 | S: Let the Blessed One teach me about suffering!” | T: Let the Blessed One teach me about suffering.”
sn12.17:5.1 | S: “Suppose that he who does the deed and he who experiences the result are one and the same. Then for one who has existed since the beginning, suffering is made by oneself. In speaking like this, one implies this is eternal. | T: “Kassapa, if one thinks, ‘The one who acts is the same as the one who experiences the result,’ then one asserts with reference to one existing from the beginning: ‘Suffering is created by oneself.’ When one asserts thus, this amounts to eternalism.
sn12.17:5.2 | S: Suppose that he who does the deed is one, and he who experiences the result is another. Then for one stricken by feeling, suffering is made by another. In speaking like this, one implies this is annihilated. | T: But, Kassapa, if one thinks, ‘The one who acts is one, the one who experiences the result is another,’ then one asserts with reference to one stricken by feeling: ‘Suffering is created by another.’ When one asserts thus, this amounts to annihilationism.
sn12.17:5.3 | S: Avoiding these two extremes, the Realized One teaches by the middle way: | T: Without veering towards either of these extremes, the Tathagata teaches the Dhamma by the middle:
sn12.17:5.4 | S: ‘Ignorance is a requirement for choices. | T: ‘With ignorance as condition, volitional formations come to be;
sn12.17:5.5 | S: Choices are a requirement for consciousness. … | T: with volitional formations as condition, consciousness….
sn12.17:5.6 | S: That is how this entire mass of suffering originates. | T: Such is the origin of this whole mass of suffering.
sn12.17:5.7 | S: When ignorance fades away and ceases with no residue left behind, choices cease. | T: But with the remainderless fading away and cessation of ignorance comes cessation of volitional formations;
sn12.17:5.8 | S: When choices cease, consciousness ceases. … | T: with the cessation of volitional formations, cessation of consciousness….
sn12.17:5.9 | S: That is how this entire mass of suffering ceases.’” | T: Such is the cessation of this whole mass of suffering.’”
sn12.17:6.1 | S: When this was said, Kassapa said to the Buddha, | T: When this was said, the naked ascetic Kassapa said to the Blessed One:
sn12.17:6.2 | S: “Excellent, sir! Excellent! | T: “Magnificent, venerable sir! Magnificent, venerable sir!
sn12.17:6.3 | S: As if he were righting the overturned, or revealing the hidden, or pointing out the path to the lost, or lighting a lamp in the dark so people with clear eyes can see what’s there, the Buddha has made the teaching clear in many ways. | T: The Dhamma has been made clear in many ways by the Blessed One, as though he were turning upright what had been turned upside down, revealing what was hidden, showing the way to one who was lost, or holding up a lamp in the dark for those with eyesight to see forms.
sn12.17:6.4 | S: I go for refuge to the Buddha, to the teaching, and to the mendicant Saṅgha. | T: I go for refuge to the Blessed One, and to the Dhamma, and to the Bhikkhu Saṅgha.
sn12.17:6.5 | S: Sir, may I receive the going forth, the ordination in the Buddha’s presence?” | T: May I receive the going forth under the Blessed One, may I receive the higher ordination?”
sn12.17:7.1 | S: “Kassapa, if someone formerly ordained in another sect wishes to take the going forth, the ordination in this teaching and training, they must spend four months on probation. When four months have passed, if the mendicants are satisfied, they’ll give the going forth, the ordination into monkhood. | T: “Kassapa, one formerly belonging to another sect who desires the going forth and the higher ordination in this Dhamma and Discipline lives on probation for four months. At the end of the four months, if the bhikkhus are satisfied with him, they may if they wish give him the going forth and the higher ordination to the state of a bhikkhu.
sn12.17:7.2 | S: However, I have recognized individual differences.” | T: But individual differences are recognized by me.”
sn12.17:8.1 | S: “Sir, if four months probation are required in such a case, I’ll spend four years on probation. When four years have passed, if the mendicants are satisfied, let them give me the going forth, the ordination into monkhood.” | T: “If, venerable sir, one formerly belonging to another sect who desires the going forth and the higher ordination in this Dhamma and Discipline lives on probation for four months, and if at the end of the four months the bhikkhus, being satisfied with him, may if they wish give him the going forth and the higher ordination to the state of a bhikkhu, then I will live on probation for four years. At the end of the four years, if the bhikkhus are satisfied with me, let them if they wish give me the going forth and the higher ordination to the state of a bhikkhu.”
sn12.17:9.1 | S: And the naked ascetic Kassapa received the going forth, the ordination in the Buddha’s presence. | T: Then the naked ascetic Kassapa received the going forth under the Blessed One, and he received the higher ordination.
sn12.17:9.2 | S: Not long after his ordination, Venerable Kassapa, living alone, withdrawn, diligent, keen, and resolute, soon realized the supreme culmination of the spiritual path in this very life. He lived having achieved with his own insight the goal for which gentlemen rightly go forth from the lay life to homelessness. | T: And soon, not long after his higher ordination, dwelling alone, withdrawn, diligent, ardent, and resolute, the Venerable Kassapa, by realizing it for himself with direct knowledge, in this very life entered and dwelt in that unsurpassed goal of the holy life for the sake of which clansmen rightly go forth from the household life into homelessness.
sn12.17:9.3 | S: He understood: “Rebirth is ended; the spiritual journey has been completed; what had to be done has been done; there is nothing further for this place.” | T: He directly knew: “Destroyed is birth, the holy life has been lived, what had to be done has been done, there is no more for this state of being.”
sn12.17:9.4 | S: And Venerable Kassapa became one of the perfected. | T: And the Venerable Kassapa became one of the arahants.
sn12.17:9.5 | P: Sattamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an10.29
an10.29:1.1 | S: “As far as Kāsi and Kosala extend, and as far as the dominion of King Pasenadi of Kosala extends, King Pasenadi is said to be the foremost. | T: (1) “Bhikkhus, as far as Kāsi and Kosala extend, as far as the realm of King Pasenadi of Kosala extends, there King Pasenadi of Kosala ranks as the foremost.
an10.29:1.2 | S: But even King Pasenadi decays and perishes. | T: But even for King Pasenadi there is alteration; there is change.
an10.29:1.3 | S: Seeing this, a learned noble disciple grows disillusioned with it. | T: Seeing this thus, the instructed noble disciple becomes disenchanted with it;
an10.29:1.4 | S: Their desire fades away even for the foremost, let alone the inferior. | T: being disenchanted, he becomes dispassionate toward the foremost, not to speak of what is inferior.
an10.29:2.1 | S: A galaxy extends a thousand times as far as the moon and sun revolve and the dazzling ones light up the quarters. | T: (2) “Bhikkhus, as far as sun and moon revolve and light up the quarters with their brightness, so far the thousandfold world system extends.
an10.29:2.2 | S: In that galaxy there are a thousand moons, a thousand suns, a thousand Sinerus king of mountains, a thousand Black Plum Tree Lands, a thousand lands west of Godāvarī, a thousand lands north of Kuru, a thousand lands east of Videha, four thousand oceans, four thousand great kings, a thousand realms of the gods of the four great kings, a thousand realms of the gods of the thirty-three, of the gods of Yama, of the joyful gods, of the gods who love to create, of the gods who control what is created by others, and a thousand realms of divinity. | T: In that thousandfold world system there are a thousand moons, a thousand suns, a thousand Sinerus king of mountains, a thousand Jambudīpas, a thousand Aparagoyānas, a thousand Uttarakurus, a thousand Pubbavidehas, and a thousand four great oceans; a thousand four great kings, a thousand heavens ruled by the four great kings, a thousand Tāvatiṁsa heavens, a thousand Yāma heavens, a thousand Tusita heavens, a thousand heavens of devas who delight in creation, a thousand heavens of devas who control what is created by others, a thousand brahmā worlds.
an10.29:2.3 | S: As far as the galaxy extends, the Great Divinity is said to be the foremost. | T: As far, bhikkhus, as this thousandfold world system extends, Mahābrahmā there ranks as the foremost.
an10.29:2.4 | S: But even the Great Divinity decays and perishes. | T: But even for Mahābrahmā there is alteration; there is change.
an10.29:2.5 | S: Seeing this, a learned noble disciple grows disillusioned with it. | T: Seeing this thus, the instructed noble disciple becomes disenchanted with it;
an10.29:2.6 | S: Their desire fades away even for the foremost, let alone the inferior. | T: being disenchanted, he becomes dispassionate toward the foremost, not to speak of what is inferior.
an10.29:3.1 | S: There comes a time when this cosmos contracts. | T: (3) “There comes a time, bhikkhus, when this world dissolves.
an10.29:3.2 | S: As it contracts, most sentient beings migrate to the realm of sublime luminosity. | T: When the world is dissolving, beings for the most part migrate to the devas of streaming radiance.
an10.29:3.3 | S: There they are mind-made, feeding on rapture, self-radiant, wandering in midair, steadily glorious, and they remain like that for a very long time. | T: There they exist mind-made, feeding on rapture, self-luminous, moving through the skies, living in glory, and they remain thus for a very long time.
an10.29:3.4 | S: When the cosmos is contracting, the gods of sublime luminosity are said to be the foremost. | T: When the world is dissolving, the devas of streaming radiance rank as the foremost.
an10.29:3.5 | S: But even the gods of sublime luminosity decay and perish. | T: But even for these devas there is alteration; there is change.
an10.29:3.6 | S: Seeing this, a learned noble disciple grows disillusioned with it. | T: Seeing this thus, the instructed noble disciple becomes disenchanted with it;
an10.29:3.7 | S: Their desire fades away even for the foremost, let alone the inferior. | T: being disenchanted, he becomes dispassionate toward the foremost, not to speak of what is inferior.
an10.29:4.1 | S: There are these ten universal dimensions of meditation. | T: (4) “Bhikkhus, there are these ten kasiṇa bases.
an10.29:4.2 | S: What ten? | T: What ten?
an10.29:4.3 | S: Someone perceives the meditation on universal earth above, below, across, undivided and limitless. | T: One person perceives the earth kasiṇa above, below, across, undivided, measureless.
an10.29:4.4 | S: They perceive the meditation on universal water … | T: One person perceives the water kasiṇa …
an10.29:4.5 | S: the meditation on universal fire … | T: the fire kasiṇa …
an10.29:4.6 | S: the meditation on universal air … | T: the air kasiṇa …
an10.29:4.7 | S: the meditation on universal blue … | T: the blue kasiṇa …
an10.29:4.8 | S: the meditation on universal yellow … | T: the yellow kasiṇa …
an10.29:4.9 | S: the meditation on universal red … | T: the red kasiṇa …
an10.29:4.10 | S: the meditation on universal white … | T: the white kasiṇa …
an10.29:4.11 | S: the meditation on universal space … | T: the space kasiṇa …
an10.29:4.12 | S: They perceive the meditation on universal consciousness above, below, across, undivided and limitless. | T: the consciousness kasiṇa above, below, across, undivided, measureless.
an10.29:4.13 | S: These are the ten universal dimensions of meditation. | T: These are the ten kasiṇa bases.
an10.29:5.1 | S: The best of these ten universal dimensions of meditation is when someone perceives the meditation on universal consciousness above, below, across, undivided and limitless. | T: Of these ten kasiṇa bases, this is the foremost, namely, when one perceives the consciousness kasiṇa above, below, across, undivided, measureless.
an10.29:5.2 | S: Some sentient beings perceive like this. | T: There are beings who are percipient in such a way.
an10.29:5.3 | S: But even the sentient beings who perceive like this decay and perish. | T: But even for beings who are percipient in such a way there is alteration; there is change.
an10.29:5.4 | S: Seeing this, a learned noble disciple grows disillusioned with it. | T: Seeing this thus, bhikkhus, the instructed noble disciple becomes disenchanted with it;
an10.29:5.5 | S: Their desire fades away even for the foremost, let alone the inferior. | T: being disenchanted, he becomes dispassionate toward the foremost, not to speak of what is inferior.
an10.29:6.1 | S: There are these eight dimensions of mastery. | T: (5) “Bhikkhus, there are these eight bases of overcoming.
an10.29:6.2 | S: What eight? | T: What eight?
an10.29:6.3 | S: Perceiving form internally, someone sees forms externally, limited, both pretty and ugly. | T: i “One percipient of forms internally sees forms externally, limited, beautiful or ugly.
an10.29:6.4 | S: Mastering them, they perceive: ‘I know and see.’ | T: Having overcome them, he is percipient thus: ‘I know, I see.’
an10.29:6.5 | S: This is the first dimension of mastery. | T: This is the first basis of overcoming.
an10.29:7.1 | S: Perceiving form internally, someone sees forms externally, limitless, both pretty and ugly. | T: ii “One percipient of forms internally sees forms externally, measureless, beautiful or ugly.
an10.29:7.2 | S: Mastering them, they perceive: ‘I know and see.’ | T: Having overcome them, he is percipient thus: ‘I know, I see.’
an10.29:7.3 | S: This is the second dimension of mastery. | T: This is the second basis of overcoming.
an10.29:8.1 | S: Not perceiving form internally, someone sees forms externally, limited, both pretty and ugly. | T: iii “One not percipient of forms internally sees forms externally, limited, beautiful or ugly.
an10.29:8.2 | S: Mastering them, they perceive: ‘I know and see.’ | T: Having overcome them, he is percipient thus: ‘I know, I see.’
an10.29:8.3 | S: This is the third dimension of mastery. | T: This is the third basis of overcoming.
an10.29:9.1 | S: Not perceiving form internally, someone sees forms externally, limitless, both pretty and ugly. | T: iv “One not percipient of forms internally sees forms externally, measureless, beautiful or ugly.
an10.29:9.2 | S: Mastering them, they perceive: ‘I know and see.’ | T: Having overcome them, he is percipient thus: ‘I know, I see.’
an10.29:9.3 | S: This is the fourth dimension of mastery. | T: This is the fourth basis of overcoming.
an10.29:10.1 | S: Not perceiving form internally, someone sees forms externally, blue, with blue color and blue appearance. | T: v “One not percipient of forms internally sees forms externally, blue ones, blue in color, with a blue hue, with a blue tint.
an10.29:10.2 | S: They’re like a flax flower that’s blue, with blue color and blue appearance. Or a cloth from Varanasi that’s smoothed on both sides, blue, with blue color and blue appearance. | T: Just as the flax flower is blue, blue in color, with a blue hue, with a blue tint, or just as Bārāṇasī cloth, smoothened on both sides, might be blue, blue in color, with a blue hue, with a blue tint,
an10.29:10.3 | S: In the same way, not perceiving form internally, someone sees forms externally, blue, with blue color and blue appearance. | T: so too, one not percipient of forms internally sees forms externally, blue ones….
an10.29:10.4 | S: Mastering them, they perceive: ‘I know and see.’ | T: Having overcome them, he is percipient thus: ‘I know, I see.’

