an10.29:15.1 | S: There are four ways of practice. | T: (6) “Bhikkhus, there are these four modes of practice.
an10.29:15.2 | S: What four? | T: What four?
an10.29:15.3 | S: Painful practice with slow insight, | T: Practice that is painful with sluggish direct knowledge;
an10.29:15.4 | S: painful practice with swift insight, | T: practice that is painful with quick direct knowledge;
an10.29:15.5 | S: pleasant practice with slow insight, and | T: practice that is pleasant with sluggish direct knowledge;
an10.29:15.6 | S: pleasant practice with swift insight. | T: and practice that is pleasant with quick direct knowledge.
an10.29:15.7 | S: These are the four ways of practice. | T: These are the four modes of practice.
an10.29:16.1 | S: The best of these four ways of practice is the pleasant practice with swift insight. | T: Of these four modes of practice, this is the foremost, namely, practice that is pleasant with quick direct knowledge.
an10.29:16.2 | S: Some sentient beings practice like this. | T: There are beings who practice in such a way.
an10.29:16.3 | S: But even the sentient beings who practice like this decay and perish. | T: But even for beings who practice in such a way there is alteration; there is change.
an10.29:16.4 | S: Seeing this, a learned noble disciple grows disillusioned with it. | T: Seeing this thus, the instructed noble disciple becomes disenchanted with it;
an10.29:16.5 | S: Their desire fades away even for the foremost, let alone the inferior. | T: being disenchanted, he becomes dispassionate toward the foremost, not to speak of what is inferior.
an10.29:17.1 | S: There are these four perceptions. | T: (7) “Bhikkhus, there are these four modes of perception.
an10.29:17.2 | S: What four? | T: What four?
an10.29:17.3 | S: One individual perceives the limited. | T: One person perceives what is limited;
an10.29:17.4 | S: One individual perceives the expansive. | T: another perceives what is exalted;
an10.29:17.5 | S: One individual perceives the limitless. | T: another perceives what is measureless;
an10.29:17.6 | S: One individual, aware that ‘there is nothing at all’, perceives the dimension of nothingness. | T: and still another, perceiving ‘There is nothing,’ perceives the base of nothingness.
an10.29:17.7 | S: These are the four perceptions. | T: These are the four modes of perception.
an10.29:18.1 | S: The best of these four perceptions is when someone, aware that ‘there is nothing at all’, perceives the dimension of nothingness. | T: Of these four modes of perception, this is the foremost, namely, when, perceiving ‘There is nothing,’ one perceives the base of nothingness.
an10.29:18.2 | S: Some sentient beings perceive like this. | T: There are beings who perceive in such a way.
an10.29:18.3 | S: But even the sentient beings who perceive like this decay and perish. | T: But even for beings who perceive in such a way there is alteration; there is change.
an10.29:18.4 | S: Seeing this, a learned noble disciple grows disillusioned with it. | T: Seeing this thus, the instructed noble disciple becomes disenchanted with it;
an10.29:18.5 | S: Their desire fades away even for the foremost, let alone the inferior. | T: being disenchanted, he becomes dispassionate toward the foremost, not to speak of what is inferior.
an10.29:19.1 | S: This is the best of the convictions of outsiders, that is: ‘I might not be, and it might not be mine. I will not be, and it will not be mine.’ | T: (8) “Bhikkhus, of the speculative views held by outsiders, this is the foremost, namely: ‘I might not be and it might not be mine; I shall not be, and it will not be mine.’
an10.29:19.2 | S: When someone has such a view, you can expect | T: For it can be expected that one who holds such a view will not be unrepelled by existence and will not be repelled by the cessation of existence.
an10.29:19.3 | S: that they will not be attracted to continued existence, | T: (none)
an10.29:19.4 | S: and they will not be repulsed by the cessation of continued existence. | T: (none)
an10.29:19.5 | S: Some sentient beings have such a view. | T: There are beings who hold such a view.
an10.29:19.6 | S: But even the sentient beings who have views like this decay and perish. | T: But even for beings who hold such a view there is alteration; there is change.
an10.29:19.7 | S: Seeing this, a learned noble disciple grows disillusioned with it. | T: Seeing this thus, the instructed noble disciple becomes disenchanted with it;
an10.29:19.8 | S: Their desire fades away even for the foremost, let alone the inferior. | T: being disenchanted, he becomes dispassionate toward the foremost, not to speak of what is inferior.
an10.29:20.1 | S: There are some ascetics and brahmins who advocate the ultimate purity of the spirit. | T: (9) “Bhikkhus, there are some ascetics and brahmins who proclaim supreme purification.
an10.29:20.2 | S: This is the best of the advocates of the ultimate purity of the spirit, that is, when someone, going totally beyond the dimension of nothingness, enters and remains in the dimension of neither perception nor non-perception. | T: Of those who proclaim supreme purification, this is the foremost, namely, by completely surmounting the base of nothingness, one enters and dwells in the base of neither-perception-nor-non-perception.
an10.29:20.3 | S: They teach Dhamma in order to directly know and realize this. | T: They teach their Dhamma for the direct knowledge and realization of this.
an10.29:20.4 | S: Some sentient beings have such a doctrine. | T: There are beings who assert thus.
an10.29:20.5 | S: But even the sentient beings who have such a doctrine decay and perish. | T: But even for those who assert thus, there is alteration; there is change.
an10.29:20.6 | S: Seeing this, a learned noble disciple grows disillusioned with it. | T: Seeing this thus, the instructed noble disciple becomes disenchanted with it;
an10.29:20.7 | S: Their desire fades away even for the foremost, let alone the inferior. | T: being disenchanted, he becomes dispassionate toward the foremost, not to speak of what is inferior.
an10.29:21.1 | S: There are some ascetics and brahmins who advocate ultimate extinguishment in this very life. | T: (10) “Bhikkhus, there are some ascetics and brahmins who proclaim supreme nibbāna in this very life.
an10.29:21.2 | S: This is the best of those who advocate extinguishment in this very life, that is, liberation by not grasping after truly understanding the origin, disappearance, gratification, drawback, and escape of the six fields of contact. | T: Of those who proclaim supreme nibbāna in this very life, this is the foremost, namely, emancipation through non-clinging after one has seen as they really are the origin and passing away, the gratification, danger, and escape in regard to the six bases for contact.
an10.29:21.3 | S: Though I state and assert this, certain ascetics and brahmins misrepresent me with the incorrect, hollow, false, untruthful claim: | T: “Bhikkhus, though I assert and declare my teaching in such a way, some ascetics and brahmins untruthfully, baselessly, falsely, and wrongly misrepresent me, by saying:
an10.29:21.4 | S: ‘The ascetic Gotama doesn’t advocate the complete understanding of sensual pleasures, forms, or feelings.’ | T: ‘The ascetic Gotama does not proclaim the full understanding of sensual pleasures, the full understanding of forms, or the full understanding of feelings.’
an10.29:21.5 | S: But I do advocate the complete understanding of sensual pleasures, forms, and feelings. And I advocate complete extinguishment with no fuel for grasping in this very life, wishless, quenched, and cooled.” | T: But, bhikkhus, I do proclaim the full understanding of sensual pleasures, the full understanding of forms, and the full understanding of feelings. In this very life, hungerless, quenched, and cooled, I proclaim final nibbāna through non-clinging.”
an10.29:21.6 | P: Navamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn4.23
sn4.23:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: Thus have I heard.
sn4.23:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Rājagaha in the Bamboo Grove, the squirrels’ feeding ground. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was dwelling at Rajagaha in the Bamboo Grove, the Squirrel Sanctuary.
sn4.23:1.3 | S: Now at that time Venerable Godhika was staying on the slopes of Isigili at the Black Rock. | T: Now on that occasion the Venerable Godhika was dwelling on the Black Rock on the Isigili Slope.
sn4.23:1.4 | S: Then Venerable Godhika, meditating diligent, keen, and resolute, experienced temporary freedom of heart. | T: Then, while the Venerable Godhika was dwelling diligent, ardent, and resolute, he reached temporary liberation of mind,
sn4.23:1.5 | S: But then he fell away from that temporary freedom of heart. | T: but he fell away from that temporary liberation of mind.
sn4.23:1.6 | S: For a second … | T: A second time, while the Venerable Godhika was dwelling diligent, ardent, and resolute, he reached temporary liberation of mind,
sn4.23:1.7 | P: Dutiyampi kho āyasmā godhiko tamhā sāmayikāya cetovimuttiyā parihāyi. | T: but he fell away from that temporary liberation of mind.
sn4.23:1.8 | S: third … | T: A third time…
sn4.23:1.9 | P: Tatiyampi kho āyasmā godhiko tamhā …pe… parihāyi. | T: (none)
sn4.23:1.10 | S: fourth … | T: A fourth time …
sn4.23:1.11 | P: Catutthampi kho āyasmā godhiko tamhā …pe… parihāyi. | T: (none)
sn4.23:1.12 | S: fifth … | T: A fifth time…
sn4.23:1.13 | P: Pañcamampi kho āyasmā …pe… vimuttiyā parihāyi. | T: (none)
sn4.23:1.14 | S: sixth time Godhika experienced temporary freedom of heart. | T: A sixth time, while the Venerable Godhika was dwelling diligent, ardent, and resolute, he reached temporary liberation of mind,
sn4.23:1.15 | S: But for a sixth time he fell away from it. | T: but he fell away from that temporary liberation of mind.
sn4.23:1.16 | S: For a seventh time Godhika, meditating diligent, keen, and resolute, experienced temporary freedom of heart. | T: A seventh time, while the Venerable Godhika was dwelling diligent, ardent, and resolute, he reached temporary liberation of mind.
sn4.23:2.1 | S: Then he thought, | T: Then it occurred to the Venerable Godhika:
sn4.23:2.2 | S: “I’ve fallen away from this temporary freedom of heart no less than six times. | T: “Six times already I have fallen away from temporary liberation of mind.
sn4.23:2.3 | S: Why don’t I commit suicide?” | T: Let me use the knife.”
sn4.23:2.4 | S: And then Māra the Wicked, knowing Godhika’s train of thought, went up to the Buddha and addressed him in verse: | T: Then Mara the Evil One, having known with his own mind the reflection in the Venerable Godhika’s mind, approached the Blessed One and addressed him with these verses:
sn4.23:3.1 | S: “O great hero, O greatly wise! | T: “O great hero, great in wisdom,
sn4.23:3.2 | S: Shining with power and glory. | T: Blazing forth with power and glory!
sn4.23:3.3 | S: You’ve gone beyond all enmities and fears, | T: (none)
sn4.23:3.4 | S: I bow to your feet, O Clear-eyed One! | T: I worship your feet, One with Vision, Who has overcome all enmity and fear.
sn4.23:4.1 | S: Great hero, master of death, | T: “O great hero who has vanquished death,
sn4.23:4.2 | S: your disciple longs for death, | T: Your disciple is longing for death.
sn4.23:4.3 | S: he’s planning for it. | T: He intends to take his own life:
sn4.23:4.4 | S: Stop him, O light-bringer! | T: Restrain him from this, O luminous one!
sn4.23:5.1 | S: For Blessed One, renowned among men, | T: “How, O Blessed One,
sn4.23:5.2 | S: your disciple who loves your teaching, | T: can your disciple— One delighting in the Teaching,
sn4.23:5.3 | S: a trainee who hasn’t achieved their heart’s desire— | T: A trainee seeking his mind’s ideal—
sn4.23:5.4 | S: how can they kill themselves?” | T: Take his own life, O widely famed?”
sn4.23:6.1 | S: Now at that time Venerable Godhika had already committed suicide. | T: Now on that occasion the Venerable Godhika had just used the knife.
sn4.23:6.2 | S: Then the Buddha, knowing that this was Māra the Wicked, addressed him in verse: | T: Then the Blessed One, having understood, “This is Mara the Evil One,” addressed him in verse:
sn4.23:7.1 | S: “This is how the attentive act, | T: “Such indeed is how the steadfast act:
sn4.23:7.2 | S: for they don’t long for life. | T: They are not attached to life.
sn4.23:7.3 | S: Having plucked out craving, root and all, | T: Having drawn out craving with its root,
sn4.23:7.4 | S: Godhika is quenched.” | T: Godhika has attained final Nibbāna.”
sn4.23:8.1 | S: Then the Buddha said to the mendicants, | T: Then the Blessed One addressed the bhikkhus thus:
sn4.23:8.2 | S: “Come, mendicants, let’s go to the Black Rock on the slopes of Isigili where Godhika, who came from a good family, committed suicide.” | T: “Come, bhikkhus, let us go to the Black Rock on the Isigili Slope, where the clansman Godhika has used the knife.”
sn4.23:8.3 | S: “Yes, sir,” they replied. | T: “Yes, venerable sir,” those bhikkhus replied.
sn4.23:9.1 | S: Then the Buddha together with several mendicants went to the Black Rock on the slopes of Isigili. | T: Then the Blessed One, together with a number of bhikkhus, went to the Black Rock on the Isigili Slope.
sn4.23:9.2 | S: The Buddha saw Godhika off in the distance on his cot, lying with aggregates cast off. | T: The Blessed One saw in the distance the Venerable Godhika lying on the bed with his shoulder turned.
sn4.23:9.3 | S: Now at that time a cloud of black smoke was moving east, west, north, south, above, below, and in-between. | T: Now on that occasion a cloud of smoke, a swirl of darkness, was moving to the east, then to the west, to the north, to the south, upwards, downwards, and to the intermediate quarters.
sn4.23:10.1 | S: Then the Buddha said to the mendicants, | T: The Blessed One then addressed the bhikkhus thus:
sn4.23:10.2 | S: “Mendicants, do you see that cloud of black smoke moving east, west, north, south, above, below, and in-between?” | T: “Do you see, bhikkhus, that cloud of smoke, that swirl of darkness, moving to the east, then to the west, to the north, to the south, upwards, downwards, and to the intermediate quarters?”
sn4.23:10.3 | S: “Yes, sir.” | T: “Yes, venerable sir.”
sn4.23:10.4 | S: “That’s Māra the Wicked searching for Godhika’s consciousness, wondering: | T: “That, bhikkhus, is Mara the Evil One searching for the consciousness of the clansman Godhika, wondering:
sn4.23:10.5 | S: ‘Where is Godhika’s consciousness established?’ | T: ‘Where now has the consciousness of the clansman Godhika been established?’
sn4.23:10.6 | S: But since his consciousness is not established, Godhika is quenched.” | T: However, bhikkhus, with consciousness unestablished, the clansman Godhika has attained final Nibbāna.”
sn4.23:10.7 | S: Then Māra, carrying his arched harp made from the pale timber of wood-apple, went up to the Buddha and addressed him in verse: | T: Then Mara the Evil One, taking a lute of yellow vilva-wood, approached the Blessed One and addressed him in verse:
sn4.23:11.1 | S: “Above, below, all round, | T: “Above, below, and across,
sn4.23:11.2 | S: in the four quarters and in-between, | T: In the four quarters and in between,
sn4.23:11.3 | S: I’ve been searching without success: | T: I have been searching but do not find
sn4.23:11.4 | S: where has that Godhika got to?” | T: Where Godhika has gone.”
sn4.23:12.1 | S: “He was an attentive and steadfast sage, | T: The Blessed One: “That steadfast man was resolute,
sn4.23:12.2 | S: a meditator who loved absorption. | T: A meditator always rejoicing in meditation,
sn4.23:12.3 | S: By day and by night he applied himself, | T: Applying himself day and night
sn4.23:12.4 | S: without concern for his life. | T: Without attachment even to life.
sn4.23:13.1 | S: He defeated the army of death, | T: “Having conquered the army of Death,
sn4.23:13.2 | S: and won’t return for any future life. | T: Not returning to renewed existence,
sn4.23:13.3 | S: Having plucked out craving, root and all | T: Having drawn out craving with its root,
sn4.23:13.4 | S: Godhika is quenched.” | T: Godhika has attained final Nibbāna.”
sn4.23:14.1 | S: So stricken with sorrow | T: So much was he stricken with sorrow
sn4.23:14.2 | S: that his harp dropped from his armpit, | T: That his lute dropped from his armpit.
sn4.23:14.3 | S: that spirit, downcast, | T: Thereupon that disappointed spirit
sn4.23:14.4 | S: vanished right there. | T: Disappeared right on the spot.

## sn1.20
sn1.20:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: Thus have I heard.
sn1.20:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Rājagaha in the Hot Springs Monastery. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was dwelling at Rājagaha in the Hot Springs Park.
sn1.20:1.3 | S: Then Venerable Samiddhi rose at the crack of dawn and went to the hot springs to bathe. | T: Then the Venerable Samiddhi, having risen at the first flush of dawn, went to the hot springs to bathe.
sn1.20:1.4 | S: When he had bathed and emerged from the water he stood in one robe drying his limbs. | T: Having bathed in the hot springs and come back out, he stood in one robe drying his limbs.
sn1.20:1.5 | S: Then, late at night, a glorious deity, lighting up the entire hot springs, went up to Samiddhi, and, standing in the air, addressed him in verse: | T: Then, when the night had advanced, a certain devatā of stunning beauty, illuminating the entire hot springs, approached the Venerable Samiddhi. Having approached, she stood in the air and addressed the Venerable Samiddhi in verse:
sn1.20:2.1 | S: “Mendicant, you seek alms before you eat; | T: “Without having enjoyed you seek alms, bhikkhu,
sn1.20:2.2 | S: you don’t seek alms after eating. | T: You don’t seek alms after you’ve enjoyed.
sn1.20:2.3 | S: But you should eat first, then seek alms: | T: First enjoy, bhikkhu, then seek alms:
sn1.20:2.4 | S: don’t let the time pass you by.” | T: Don’t let the time pass you by!”
sn1.20:3.1 | S: “I actually don’t know the time; | T: “I do not know what the time might be;
sn1.20:3.2 | S: it’s hidden and unseen. | T: The time is hidden and cannot be seen.
sn1.20:3.3 | S: That’s why I seek alms before eating, | T: Hence, without enjoying, I seek alms:
sn1.20:3.4 | S: so that the time may not pass me by!” | T: Don’t let the time pass me by!”
sn1.20:4.1 | S: Then that deity landed on the ground and said to Samiddhi, | T: Then that devatā alighted on the earth and said to the Venerable Samiddhi:
sn1.20:4.2 | S: “You’ve gone forth while young, mendicant. With pristine black hair, you’re blessed with youth, in the prime of life, and you’ve never flirted with sensual pleasures. | T: “You have gone forth while young, bhikkhu, a lad with black hair, endowed with the blessing of youth, in the prime of life, without having dallied with sensual pleasures.
sn1.20:4.3 | S: Enjoy human sensual pleasures! | T: Enjoy human sensual pleasures, bhikkhu;
sn1.20:4.4 | S: Don’t give up what is apparent in the present to chase after what takes effect over time.” | T: do not abandon what is directly visible in order to pursue what takes time.”
sn1.20:5.1 | S: “I’m not, respectable sir. | T: “I have not abandoned what is directly visible, friend, in order to pursue what takes time.
sn1.20:5.2 | S: I’m giving up what takes effect over time to chase after what is apparent in the present. | T: I have abandoned what takes time in order to pursue what is directly visible.
sn1.20:5.3 | S: For the Buddha has said that sensual pleasures take effect over time, with much suffering and distress, and they’re all the more full of drawbacks. | T: For the Blessed One, friend, has stated that sensual pleasures are time-consuming, full of suffering, full of despair, and the danger in them is still greater,
sn1.20:5.4 | S: But this teaching is apparent in the present life, immediately effective, inviting inspection, relevant, so that sensible people can know it for themselves.” | T: while this Dhamma is directly visible, immediate, inviting one to come and see, applicable, to be personally experienced by the wise.”
sn1.20:6.1 | S: “But in what way, mendicant, has the Buddha said that sensual pleasures take effect over time, with much suffering and distress, and they’re all the more full of drawbacks? | T: “But how is it, bhikkhu, that the Blessed One has stated that sensual pleasures are time-consuming, full of suffering, full of despair, and the danger in them is still greater?
sn1.20:6.2 | S: And how is this teaching apparent in the present life, immediately effective, inviting inspection, relevant, so that sensible people can know it for themselves?” | T: How is it that this Dhamma is directly visible, immediate, inviting one to come and see, applicable, to be personally experienced by the wise?”
sn1.20:7.1 | S: “I’m junior, respectable sir, recently gone forth, newly come to this teaching and training. | T: “I am newly ordained, friend, not long gone forth, just recently come to this Dhamma and Discipline.
sn1.20:7.2 | S: I’m not able to explain this in detail. | T: I cannot explain it in detail.
sn1.20:7.3 | S: But he, that Blessed One, the perfected one, the fully awakened Buddha, is staying near Rājagaha in the Hot Springs Monastery. | T: But that Blessed One, the Arahant, the Perfectly Enlightened One, is dwelling at Rajagaha in the Hot Springs Park.
sn1.20:7.4 | S: You should approach him and ask about this matter. | T: Approach that Blessed One and ask him about this matter.
sn1.20:7.5 | S: And you should remember it in line with the Buddha’s answer.” | T: As he explains it to you, so you should remember it.”
sn1.20:8.1 | S: “It’s not easy for us to approach the Buddha, as he is surrounded by other illustrious deities. | T: “It isn’t easy for us to approach that Blessed One, bhikkhu, as he is surrounded by other devatās of great influence.
sn1.20:8.2 | S: If you go to the Buddha and ask him about this matter, we’ll come along and listen to the teaching.” | T: If you would approach him and ask him about this matter, we will come along too in order to hear the Dhamma.”
sn1.20:8.3 | S: “Yes, reverend,” Venerable Samiddhi replied. He went to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, and told him what had happened. Then he added: | T: “Very well, friend,” the Venerable Samiddhi replied. Then he approached the Blessed One, paid homage to him, sat down to one side, and reported his entire discussion with that devatā, verses 44–45, included in the report, repeat verses 42–43 adding:
sn1.20:9.1 | P: “Idhāhaṁ, bhante, rattiyā paccūsasamayaṁ paccuṭṭhāya yena tapodā tenupasaṅkamiṁ gattāni parisiñcituṁ. | T: (none)
sn1.20:9.2 | P: Tapode gattāni parisiñcitvā paccuttaritvā ekacīvaro aṭṭhāsiṁ gattāni pubbāpayamāno. | T: (none)
sn1.20:9.3 | P: Atha kho, bhante, aññatarā devatā abhikkantāya rattiyā abhikkantavaṇṇā kevalakappaṁ tapodaṁ obhāsetvā yenāhaṁ tenupasaṅkami; upasaṅkamitvā vehāsaṁ ṭhitā imāya gāthāya ajjhabhāsi: | T: (none)
sn1.20:10.1 | P: ‘Abhutvā bhikkhasi bhikkhu, | T: (none)
sn1.20:10.2 | P: na hi bhutvāna bhikkhasi; | T: (none)
sn1.20:10.3 | P: Bhutvāna bhikkhu bhikkhassu, | T: (none)
sn1.20:10.4 | P: mā taṁ kālo upaccagā’ti. | T: (none)
sn1.20:11.1 | P: Evaṁ vutte, ahaṁ, bhante, taṁ devataṁ gāthāya paccabhāsiṁ: | T: (none)
sn1.20:12.1 | P: ‘Kālaṁ vohaṁ na jānāmi, | T: (none)
sn1.20:12.2 | P: channo kālo na dissati; | T: (none)

