## sn12.17
sn12.17:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: Thus have I heard.
sn12.17:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Rājagaha in the Bamboo Grove, the squirrels’ feeding ground. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was dwelling at Rajagaha in the Bamboo Grove, the Squirrel Sanctuary.
sn12.17:1.3 | S: Then the Buddha robed up in the morning and, taking his bowl and robe, entered Rājagaha for alms. | T: Then, in the morning, the Blessed One dressed and, taking bowl and robe, entered Rajagaha for alms.
sn12.17:1.4 | S: The naked ascetic Kassapa saw the Buddha coming off in the distance. | T: The naked ascetic Kassapa saw the Blessed One coming in the distance.
sn12.17:1.5 | S: He went up to the Buddha, and exchanged greetings with him. | T: Having seen him, he approached the Blessed One and exchanged greetings with him.
sn12.17:1.6 | S: When the greetings and polite conversation were over, he stood to one side and said to the Buddha, | T: When they had concluded their greetings and cordial talk, he stood to one side and said to him:
sn12.17:1.7 | S: “I’d like to ask the worthy Gotama about a certain point, if you’d take the time to answer.” | T: “We would like to ask Master Gotama about a certain point, if he would grant us the favour of answering our question.”
sn12.17:1.8 | S: “Kassapa, it’s the wrong time for questions. | T: “This is not the right time for a question, Kassapa.
sn12.17:1.9 | S: We’ve entered an inhabited area.” | T: We have entered among the houses.”
sn12.17:2.1 | S: A second time, | T: A second time and a third time the naked ascetic Kassapa said to the Blessed One:
sn12.17:2.2 | P: “puccheyyāma mayaṁ bhavantaṁ gotamaṁ kañcideva desaṁ, sace no bhavaṁ gotamo okāsaṁ karoti pañhassa veyyākaraṇāyā”ti. | T: “We would like to ask Master Gotama about a certain point, if he would grant us the favour of answering our question.”
sn12.17:2.3 | P: “Akālo kho tāva, kassapa, pañhassa; | T: “This is not the right time for a question, Kassapa. We have entered among the houses.”
sn12.17:2.4 | P: antaragharaṁ paviṭṭhamhā”ti. | T: (none)
sn12.17:2.5 | S: and a third time, Kassapa spoke to the Buddha and the Buddha replied. | T: (none)
sn12.17:2.6 | P: antaragharaṁ paviṭṭhamhāti. | T: (none)
sn12.17:2.7 | S: When this was said, Kassapa said to the Buddha, | T: Then the naked ascetic Kassapa said to the Blessed One:
sn12.17:2.8 | S: “I don’t want to ask much.” | T: “We do not wish to ask Master Gotama much.”
sn12.17:2.9 | S: “Ask what you wish, Kassapa.” | T: “Then ask what you want, Kassapa.”
sn12.17:3.1 | S: “Well, worthy Gotama, is suffering made by oneself?” | T: “How is it, Master Gotama: is suffering created by oneself?”
sn12.17:3.2 | S: “Not so, Kassapa,” said the Buddha. | T: “Not so, Kassapa,” the Blessed One said.
sn12.17:3.3 | S: “Then is suffering made by another?” | T: “Then, Master Gotama, is suffering created by another?”
sn12.17:3.4 | S: “Not so, Kassapa,” said the Buddha. | T: “Not so, Kassapa,” the Blessed One said.
sn12.17:3.5 | S: “Well, is suffering made by both oneself and another?” | T: “How is it then, Master Gotama: is suffering created both by oneself and by another?”
sn12.17:3.6 | S: “Not so, Kassapa,” said the Buddha. | T: “Not so, Kassapa,” the Blessed One said.
sn12.17:3.7 | S: “Then does suffering arise anomalously, not made by oneself or another?” | T: “Then, Master Gotama, has suffering arisen fortuitously, being created neither by oneself nor by another?”
sn12.17:3.8 | S: “Not so, Kassapa,” said the Buddha. | T: “Not so, Kassapa,” the Blessed One said.
sn12.17:3.9 | S: “Well, is there no such thing as suffering?” | T: “How is it then, Master Gotama: is there no suffering?”
sn12.17:3.10 | S: “It’s not that there’s no such thing as suffering. | T: “It is not that there is no suffering, Kassapa;

